I've written two task files for this session and the issue responses file.

Task 1: GPT-6.2 tool calls (product, Part of #1060). With --model gpt-6.2-sol, yoyo sends tool calls to Chat Completions. OpenAI's GPT-6.2 guide says tool calling there needs the Responses API, so every tool call on Codex's current default model breaks in yoyo.
• The change: any gpt-6 id that yoyo doesn't already match exactly gets built with yoagent's ModelConfig::generic, which sends it to Responses. That means reusing yoagent's own check instead of writing another hand list. I looked at yoagent 0.27.9 to confirm generic sends these ids to Responses.
• Model list: the GPT-6.2 ids get added to the openai model list, which currently has no GPT-6 entries at all.
• Testing: wire tests run against the local stub only, never api.openai.com, with a near-miss row and a positive control.
• Left out on purpose: gpt-5.x and the o-series stay on Chat Completions. OpenAI's docs have only been checked for GPT-6.2, so that part of #1060 stays open.

Task 2: rm with `..` (product, Fixes #1236). This closes the open self-filed issue with the least work left (18 are open, over the threshold of 12). It covers two cases where yoyo reads the path as text and the real delete goes somewhere else:
• Symlink before `..`: rm -rf /tmp/link/../* passes today, but if /tmp/link points at /, it deletes /*. The fix resolves the part before the first .. on disk and keeps the stricter of the two readings.
• Root's home: for a user whose home is /root, rm -rf ~/../* deletes /* but today only asks. The fix puts in the real $HOME value, so it is blocked like /*.
• Testing: the check becomes a function the tests can call directly, so they never change HOME or run rm. The commit will say plainly that the symlink can change between the check and the run, so this narrows the gap rather than closing it.

Neither task touches context.rs or format/mod.rs, which are both close to their size limits. I'm skipping #1233, #1215, #1239 and #1202 because they're waiting on @yuanhao, and there's no release because one isn't due.

The commit step did nothing: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the files exist on disk but aren't committed. I assumed the next phase reads them straight from the folder and didn't force-add them.
